The Kethari are serpentine desert peoples of the Saltglass Reach whose political memory predates Keeper survey records.
They are witnesses more than conquerors: territorial authority is based on proven knowledge of subsurface water, migration cycles, and long-term terrain change.
Their foundational event is the Aquifer Covenant at the Great Oasis, a living recitation that allocates Deepwell Confluence claims and dispute rules between Kethari communities.
The Covenant is not a written charter. It is renewed orally by elder witnesses, making continuity of memory itself a legal institution.
Kethari communities mapped water fractures and route safety before the Keepers formalized desert administration, and many later road decisions followed Kethari precedents.
Their venom is real but secondary to their strategic value: they are the most reliable experts on aquifer integrity, salt-crust stability, and long-horizon desert risk.
In modern politics they remain key arbiters in Saltglass water disputes, especially where civic ledgers conflict with lived hydrology.
